.TH auto-restore-ownership 8

\" auto-man2man (used in generating subcommand lists) depends on this section
.SH NAME
.PP
auto-restore-ownership - Restore ownership of files

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-restore-ownership user old-passwword-file old-group-file directory
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
user                The username of the recreated user
old-password-file   The password file containing the old user account
old-group-file      The group file containing the old groups
directory           The directory in which to update ownership
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-restore-ownership
command restores ownership of files in "directory" for "user"
using information provided in "old-password-file" and "old-group-file".
I.e., files owned by the userid and groupid in the old password
and group files are chown'd to the new userid and groupid of the
user.  This is useful after an account has been removed and
recreated without preserving userid and groupid.

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-restore-ownership joeuser passwd.old group.old /home/joeuser
.ad
.fi

.SH FILES
.nf
.na
/etc/passwd, /etc/group
.ad
.fi

.SH SEE ALSO

auto-admin(8), auto-restore-user(8), auto-restore-pw(8)

